// Arc: puzzle (four siblings, one pair of parents), then the halving, then the shuffle. Hero: the
// four McClintock children, about 1907 (public domain). Sim: the cross archetype filled with data
// only, tracking chromosome copies instead of traits: at one spot, S is the copy each parent gave the
// eldest child and N the copy it kept back, so the one-gene cross counts what younger siblings share.
// Weismann's 1887 essay is quoted from Gutenberg #48132 exactly. A second pea-cross beat, "Two spots,
// one egg", turns the same engine to independent assortment: its two "parents" are one spot on each of
// two chromosome pairs in one woman, and each egg takes one copy at each spot (a quarter, a half and a
// quarter). It is its own beat, not a third step of "Deal the copies", because that beat is pinned
// byte for byte by cases/cross-compat.test.tsx. The cases use the cross engine's second gene
// (cases/types.ts SecondGene, docs/dihybrid-design.md §4), so their answers are sixteenths that no
// share the lesson prints can reach. Single spots keep crossing over out of every count; it stays in
// the documentary, the order beat and the bank: the cross engine can't run a crossover.

